WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:11.100
This is Chapter 2: Microsoft 365
Message center and service health.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.837
Message center already tells a
tenant what will change and when.

5
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:18.180
The work is reading it from
the API, on the right cloud,

6
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.900
and saying who decided
a post needs action.

7
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.885
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's
service communications API,

8
00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:29.713
see what it leaves to you, then read how
the kit fills the record from Chapter 1,

9
00:00:29.713 --> 00:00:32.625
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:34.200
Two notes before we begin.

11
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.562
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

12
00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:41.440
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.303
Why this chapter?

14
00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:49.199
Message center is where
Microsoft tells a tenant

15
00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:52.165
about retirements and
changes, often months ahead.

16
00:00:52.165 --> 00:00:54.079
But a post that nobody reads,

17
00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.990
or that only arrives by email,
doesn't become anyone's task.

18
00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.740
So the goal is precise.

19
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.339
By the end you can read a tenant's Message
center posts and service health issues

20
00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:08.521
with just two read permissions, on the
host for the tenant's own cloud, into

21
00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:13.190
records that say which posts need action
and whether Microsoft or the kit said so.

22
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.238
This is for the developers, the
Microsoft 365 service owner,

23
00:01:17.238 --> 00:01:20.740
and the change manager
who receives the tasks.

24
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.002
First, which cloud.

25
00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:29.906
A GCC tenant keeps the worldwide
Microsoft Graph endpoint,

26
00:01:29.906 --> 00:01:33.969
so this workbook's baseline reads
graph dot microsoft dot com.

27
00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:37.953
Microsoft Learn's national cloud
page gives GCC High and the

28
00:01:37.953 --> 00:01:41.777
Defense Department their own
hosts, a short side note here.

29
00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:42.864
Why does it matter?

30
00:01:42.864 --> 00:01:45.177
Because a wrong cloud fails silently.

31
00:01:45.177 --> 00:01:49.848
A GCC High tenant read on the
worldwide host simply returns nothing,

32
00:01:49.848 --> 00:01:52.266
and the gate passes on an empty list.

33
00:01:52.266 --> 00:01:55.022
So the kit has one row
per cloud and no default,

34
00:01:55.022 --> 00:02:00.450
and the live run refuses a Graph session
in another environment or another tenant.

35
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.075
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

36
00:02:06.075 --> 00:02:11.239
Microsoft Learn's service communications
API overview names the resources and

37
00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:16.325
the two permissions: one to read Message
center posts, one to read service health.

38
00:02:16.325 --> 00:02:18.944
Both are granted only by an administrator,

39
00:02:18.944 --> 00:02:21.862
and a delegated sign-in
also needs an admin role.

40
00:02:21.862 --> 00:02:26.303
There are three list methods on version
one point oh: messages, issues and

41
00:02:26.303 --> 00:02:31.262
health overviews, each with its permission
and its national-cloud availability.

42
00:02:31.262 --> 00:02:35.703
One caveat from Learn's admin roles
page: data privacy messages are

43
00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:40.788
visible only to Global Administrators
and Message Center Privacy Readers.

44
00:02:40.788 --> 00:02:45.920
And Learn's own cmdlet for posts is the
one we'll cross-check against in Lab 2.

45
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.033
What does Learn leave to you?

46
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:56.601
No post category says retirement, so the
kit treats a post tagged Retirement as one;

47
00:02:56.601 --> 00:03:00.695
Learn doesn't list the tags, so
that's marked not yet specified.

48
00:03:00.695 --> 00:03:04.416
The action-required date is
the only field where Microsoft

49
00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:07.757
says it wants action, so the
kit keeps it as Microsoft's.

50
00:03:07.757 --> 00:03:12.721
Samples show enum values in a
different case, post bodies are HTML,

51
00:03:12.721 --> 00:03:18.144
a next link is a URL the response chose,
and a post past its end still lists.

52
00:03:18.144 --> 00:03:20.170
Each gap is pinned by a test.

53
00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.657
The verdict: use Learn's calls
and permissions as they are,

54
00:03:23.657 --> 00:03:27.360
and build the kinds, the action
basis and the host guard.

55
00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.048
Now the code.

56
00:03:32.048 --> 00:03:36.257
The Microsoft 365 collector
makes three declared calls:

57
00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:39.123
messages, issues and health overviews.

58
00:03:39.123 --> 00:03:41.780
In a live run each is
a GET through the Graph

59
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.122
PowerShell request cmdlet;
in fixture mode it's a file.

60
00:03:45.122 --> 00:03:47.947
The posts and the issues become notices.

61
00:03:47.947 --> 00:03:52.522
The health overviews don't; they're kept
in the raw folder, for the run's record.

62
00:03:52.522 --> 00:03:57.465
And the source link of every record points
to the post or issue on the same host the

63
00:03:57.465 --> 00:04:02.780
kit read it from, so a task always links
back to what Microsoft actually published.

64
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:08.468
Here's how the normalizer maps them.

65
00:04:08.468 --> 00:04:11.006
A post tagged Retirement is a retirement.

66
00:04:11.006 --> 00:04:14.293
Plan-for-change and
prevent-or-fix posts are changes,

67
00:04:14.293 --> 00:04:16.817
and stay-informed posts are advisories.

68
00:04:16.817 --> 00:04:20.413
For all of them, if Microsoft
set an action-required date,

69
00:04:20.413 --> 00:04:23.618
the record keeps it, and
says the provider decided.

70
00:04:23.618 --> 00:04:28.974
There's one kit rule: a prevent-or-fix post
with no date is still action required,

71
00:04:28.974 --> 00:04:32.154
labeled as the kit's rule,
with no date invented.

72
00:04:32.154 --> 00:04:35.809
Service health issues become
incidents or advisories,

73
00:04:35.809 --> 00:04:39.660
never action required, and
a resolved issue is closed.

74
00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.998
Because a wrong cloud fails silently,
the kit guards the host three ways.

75
00:04:47.998 --> 00:04:52.880
First, it can build each live request
from the settings without running it,

76
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.961
so a reviewer sees the exact URL.

77
00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:58.523
Second, before the first call
it compares the Graph session's

78
00:04:58.523 --> 00:05:02.372
environment and tenant with the
settings, and refuses a mismatch.

79
00:05:02.372 --> 00:05:06.833
Third, it follows a next link only if
it's on the cloud's own Graph host,

80
00:05:06.833 --> 00:05:09.347
so the token never goes anywhere else.

81
00:05:09.347 --> 00:05:11.998
Tests cover the collector and each guard.

82
00:05:11.998 --> 00:05:14.561
And remember, the live reader was tested

83
00:05:14.561 --> 00:05:17.790
against stand-in responses,
not a live tenant.

84
00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.590
Who's involved?

85
00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.690
Developers maintain the collector.

86
00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.778
The Microsoft 365 service
owner owns this gate.

87
00:05:28.778 --> 00:05:32.366
The change manager receives the
change and retirement tasks,

88
00:05:32.366 --> 00:05:36.377
and the security officer keeps the
consent record and the run folder.

89
00:05:36.377 --> 00:05:40.652
What the reader may hold is just two read
permissions, and nothing that writes.

90
00:05:40.652 --> 00:05:44.976
Learn says both are granted only by
an administrator, and a delegated

91
00:05:44.976 --> 00:05:49.603
reader also needs an admin role; which
roles Graph accepts isn't specified.

92
00:05:49.603 --> 00:05:53.715
One gap to plan for: data privacy
posts need a Global Administrator

93
00:05:53.715 --> 00:05:58.030
or a Message Center Privacy Reader,
so name one where they matter.

94
00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.280
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, working in the kit's folder.

95
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:11.368
The lab settings name a fictional
tenant in GCC; nothing signs in.

96
00:06:11.368 --> 00:06:14.204
Step 1 lists what Microsoft 365 needs:

97
00:06:14.204 --> 00:06:18.779
two permissions across three
calls, and nothing that writes.

98
00:06:18.779 --> 00:06:23.824
Step 2 prints each cloud's Graph
host: GCC on the worldwide host

99
00:06:23.824 --> 00:06:28.868
with the Global environment, then
GCC High and DoD on their own.

100
00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:31.505
There's one row per cloud and no default.

101
00:06:31.505 --> 00:06:36.883
Step 3 collects Microsoft 365 from
the day-one fixtures: six notices,

102
00:06:36.883 --> 00:06:41.850
five open, two needing action,
all valid against the schema.

103
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.550
Step 4 reads the records.

104
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:53.534
The post tagged Retirement, about basic
authentication for client submission,

105
00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:56.163
is a retirement due January 31, 2027.

106
00:06:56.163 --> 00:07:00.413
The SharePoint Framework post is a
change that needs action with no date.

107
00:07:00.413 --> 00:07:02.900
And the resolved Teams issue is closed.

108
00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.938
Step 5 shows who said
each one needs action.

109
00:07:05.938 --> 00:07:08.207
The retirement's date is Microsoft's:

110
00:07:08.207 --> 00:07:11.537
the basis names the provider's
action-required field.

111
00:07:11.537 --> 00:07:15.426
The change's basis is the kit's
rule for a prevent-or-fix post.

112
00:07:15.426 --> 00:07:17.050
Everything else says none.

113
00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.850
That line is what keeps a kit rule from
being mistaken for Microsoft's deadline.

114
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.962
Step 6 collects the second
week and reads what changed.

115
00:07:28.962 --> 00:07:30.401
Seven notices now.

116
00:07:30.401 --> 00:07:36.663
A new post arrives with its own action
date from Microsoft, February 28, 2027.

117
00:07:36.663 --> 00:07:40.250
The SharePoint post is updated,
and its title says so.

118
00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:43.337
The email incident from
week one is now closed.

119
00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:46.867
Step 7 builds the live
request without sending it:

120
00:07:46.867 --> 00:07:52.463
the first line is the exact URL a live run
would call, on the worldwide Graph host.

121
00:07:52.463 --> 00:07:55.244
Then it tries a next link on another host,

122
00:07:55.244 --> 00:07:59.640
and the kit refuses it, because
it isn't on the GCC Graph host.

123
00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.982
Step 8 runs this chapter's tests:

124
00:08:05.982 --> 00:08:11.965
the Microsoft 365 collector block and
every Graph test in the live tests.

125
00:08:11.965 --> 00:08:14.040
Fifteen pass, none fail.

126
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:17.277
The manifest lists the three
calls with their permissions.

127
00:08:17.277 --> 00:08:22.041
So, in one short lab, you've seen two
read permissions and nothing that writes,

128
00:08:22.041 --> 00:08:26.881
one Graph host per cloud with no default,
Microsoft's date kept as Microsoft's

129
00:08:26.881 --> 00:08:31.491
and the kit's rule labeled as a rule,
and a next link on another host refused.

130
00:08:31.491 --> 00:08:34.099
Delete the output folder when you're done,

131
00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:37.380
so no fixture output is
mistaken for a real run.

132
00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:46.623
Lab 2 uses a Microsoft 365 test
tenant in the worldwide or GCC cloud,

133
00:08:46.623 --> 00:08:50.917
never production, signed in
as a test administrator.

134
00:08:50.917 --> 00:08:54.066
Sign in delegated, in
the Global environment,

135
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:58.405
with only the two read scopes;
an administrator consents once.

136
00:08:58.405 --> 00:09:02.892
Cross-check with Learn's own cmdlet: its
count of posts and the kit's should agree.

137
00:09:02.892 --> 00:09:05.283
Then look at the posts that need action,

138
00:09:05.283 --> 00:09:08.422
confirm each has Microsoft's
basis or the kit's rule,

139
00:09:08.422 --> 00:09:13.279
and record the tags you see on retirement
posts, because Learn doesn't list them.

140
00:09:13.279 --> 00:09:19.680
Finally, sign out, delete the run, and remove
any consent granted only for this lab.

141
00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:28.230
Each output is evidence for something
specific, against NIST's control catalog.

142
00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.759
Two read permissions
and nothing that writes

143
00:09:30.759 --> 00:09:33.555
support monitoring an
external service provider.

144
00:09:33.555 --> 00:09:38.841
Reads that went only to the tenant's own
Graph host, and a run folder showing every

145
00:09:38.841 --> 00:09:44.367
post and issue was read, support that and
receiving security alerts and advisories.

146
00:09:44.367 --> 00:09:48.878
And each change or retirement post,
marked with who said it needs action,

147
00:09:48.878 --> 00:09:53.693
supports configuration change control,
through the tasks Chapter 6 routes.

148
00:09:53.693 --> 00:09:59.167
The indicators are FedRAMP's key security
indicators; the mapping is the program's.

149
00:09:59.167 --> 00:10:03.620
The kit's records support these
controls; the assessor decides them.

150
00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:08.770
What breaks in production?

151
00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:14.429
Read a GCC High or DoD tenant on the
worldwide host and nothing comes back,

152
00:10:14.429 --> 00:10:17.070
so the gate passes on an empty list.

153
00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.857
Follow a next link to another host and the
token goes somewhere you didn't choose.

154
00:10:21.857 --> 00:10:26.344
Grant a broad Graph scope for convenience
and the reader can change the tenant.

155
00:10:26.344 --> 00:10:30.995
Mistake the kit's rule for Microsoft's
deadline and a post gets an invented date.

156
00:10:30.995 --> 00:10:34.457
Data privacy posts go unseen
without the right role.

157
00:10:34.457 --> 00:10:39.219
And trusting the email over the API
means look-alike email gets acted on,

158
00:10:39.219 --> 00:10:43.280
so the task always links the
post the kit read from Graph.

159
00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.380
Here's what done looks like.

160
00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.143
The settings name the tenant's cloud and
tenant ID, and the session guard passed.

161
00:10:53.143 --> 00:10:57.179
The reader holds the two read permissions
and nothing else from this kit.

162
00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:00.880
Learn's cmdlet and the kit's
collection agree in the test tenant.

163
00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:05.718
Every action-required post says whether
Microsoft or the kit's rule decided it.

164
00:11:05.718 --> 00:11:09.593
The retirement tags you saw are
recorded in the verification log.

165
00:11:09.593 --> 00:11:12.981
And consent granted only for
the lab has been removed.

166
00:11:12.981 --> 00:11:15.583
Next, Chapter 3 fills the same record from

167
00:11:15.583 --> 00:11:18.568
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure's
announcements.

168
00:11:18.568 --> 00:11:19.720
Thanks for watching.
